WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.620 --> 00:00:08.783
Knife-making is almost meditative for me.

00:00:10.370 --> 00:00:11.290
There's a lot of passion behind it.

00:00:11.290 --> 00:00:14.080
There's a lot of hard work behind it.

00:00:14.080 --> 00:00:16.593
And it's not just something
that you kind of just brush off,

00:00:16.593 --> 00:00:18.503
like, oh, this is just a knife.

00:00:20.120 --> 00:00:21.420
People hands touched this knife.

00:00:21.420 --> 00:00:23.830
People pour their passion and their soul

00:00:23.830 --> 00:00:25.673
inside of these products.

00:00:26.850 --> 00:00:31.830
The art of blacksmithing
has a very long history.

00:00:31.830 --> 00:00:34.310
Our notion of what it
means to be civilized

00:00:34.310 --> 00:00:35.920
is to have metallurgy,

00:00:35.920 --> 00:00:40.873
to have the ability to
transform the natural world.

00:00:42.180 --> 00:00:43.180
When somebody use my knife,

00:00:43.180 --> 00:00:45.940
I want them to say, damn.

00:00:45.940 --> 00:00:48.840
Golly, this is a badass knife.

00:00:48.840 --> 00:00:50.790
Come here, let me show you all something.

00:00:59.690 --> 00:01:01.765
Well, let me tell you what
Quintin Middleton does.

00:01:01.765 --> 00:01:03.783
I make knives for a living.

00:01:04.830 --> 00:01:05.663
Who knew?

00:01:08.170 --> 00:01:10.530
I am from St. Stephen, South Carolina.

00:01:10.530 --> 00:01:12.640
That's maybe about an
hour away from Charleston,

00:01:12.640 --> 00:01:13.473
South Carolina.

00:01:13.473 --> 00:01:15.163
We are basically in my backyard.

00:01:18.510 --> 00:01:19.420
This is my studio.

00:01:19.420 --> 00:01:20.750
This is where it all goes down.

00:01:20.750 --> 00:01:23.233
Where every last one of my knives is made.

00:01:25.080 --> 00:01:28.320
Why does Quintin Middleton make knives?

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:29.355
Because I love it.

00:01:29.355 --> 00:01:33.130
It's just a boy-ish love
from watching fantasy movies

00:01:33.130 --> 00:01:35.750
like "Star Wars, He-Man, Conan."

00:01:35.750 --> 00:01:37.611
It just piqued my imagination.

00:01:37.611 --> 00:01:42.611
I have the power.

00:01:43.210 --> 00:01:45.850
I've watched him go from
making Kung Fu weapons

00:01:45.850 --> 00:01:49.030
out of lawnmower blades to chef's knives,

00:01:49.030 --> 00:01:52.290
to being a serious businessman,

00:01:52.290 --> 00:01:54.080
like a force to be reckoned with.

00:01:55.210 --> 00:01:56.043
I love color.

00:01:56.043 --> 00:01:56.960
I love pops of color.

00:01:56.960 --> 00:01:59.340
I love the reds, the blues, the greens.

00:01:59.340 --> 00:02:00.780
That's my personality.

00:02:00.780 --> 00:02:03.301
And I like my knives to look sexy.

00:02:05.030 --> 00:02:06.650
The knife that we'll be making today

00:02:06.650 --> 00:02:07.921
will be an eight inch chef knife.

00:02:07.921 --> 00:02:09.840
It will be damascus.

00:02:09.840 --> 00:02:13.140
Damascus steel is a set of techniques

00:02:13.140 --> 00:02:18.140
that involve the layering of
iron upon iron, upon iron,

00:02:18.420 --> 00:02:23.420
creating this more complex
and more durable structure.

00:02:24.900 --> 00:02:26.950
I will be using two types of steel.

00:02:26.950 --> 00:02:28.460
It basically is going to give you contrast

00:02:28.460 --> 00:02:29.855
between shiny and dark.

00:02:29.855 --> 00:02:31.610
You will see a bunch of swirls in it.

00:02:31.610 --> 00:02:32.960
That's what make it pretty.

00:02:36.690 --> 00:02:40.180
First, I am measuring each
one of these pieces of steel.

00:02:40.180 --> 00:02:43.150
Each one of them is cut at five inches.

00:02:43.150 --> 00:02:45.763
And then I'm chopping
it up in the chop saw.

00:02:53.170 --> 00:02:55.580
I take it next to my grinder

00:02:57.100 --> 00:03:00.230
and I'm cleaning the
surface of the billets.

00:03:00.230 --> 00:03:02.430
So when I put it in the forge,

00:03:02.430 --> 00:03:04.230
it has a clean surface to adhere to.

00:03:05.420 --> 00:03:08.350
I'm going to stack about 10 layers,

00:03:08.350 --> 00:03:10.003
and I'm going to weld it together.

00:03:15.120 --> 00:03:18.013
Now we're going to put it into a forge.

00:03:21.220 --> 00:03:24.160
And the forge is about 2,300 degrees.

00:03:24.160 --> 00:03:27.410
And basically that's not
molting, but very close.

00:03:27.410 --> 00:03:29.533
So I need it to stick to each other.

00:03:30.900 --> 00:03:33.000
I'm looking for a particular color.

00:03:33.000 --> 00:03:36.400
The color I'm looking for is
like a bright yellowish orange.

00:03:36.400 --> 00:03:39.370
So once it reach the
color I'm looking for,

00:03:39.370 --> 00:03:41.740
it's time to pull it out
'cause I don't want it to burn

00:03:41.740 --> 00:03:42.573
or melt.

00:03:45.240 --> 00:03:47.830
Next we're going to
use the hydraulic press

00:03:47.830 --> 00:03:48.910
and we're going to compress it.

00:03:48.910 --> 00:03:52.080
So this is now kneading
the steel like dough.

00:03:52.080 --> 00:03:55.240
And I'm not going to initially
just chomp down on it.

00:03:55.240 --> 00:03:57.650
I'm doing light touches.

00:03:57.650 --> 00:04:00.210
And once we do that and we
get a long enough piece,

00:04:00.210 --> 00:04:02.153
we're going to cut it in half.

00:04:05.270 --> 00:04:06.810
The piece that I cut off,
I'm going to stack it

00:04:06.810 --> 00:04:09.890
to the other piece,
building up those layers.

00:04:09.890 --> 00:04:12.320
And we're going to do
the same thing again.

00:04:12.320 --> 00:04:13.940
Put it in the forge, heat it up.

00:04:13.940 --> 00:04:16.790
Draw it out on the hydraulic press.

00:04:16.790 --> 00:04:18.140
And I'm going to cut it again.

00:04:19.750 --> 00:04:22.410
So basically now we have
a great starting point

00:04:22.410 --> 00:04:24.393
to what the knife is going to look like.

00:04:28.280 --> 00:04:31.530
Historically, blacksmithing
played an important role

00:04:31.530 --> 00:04:35.440
in the formation of
African-American identity.

00:04:35.440 --> 00:04:37.420
During the transatlantic slave trade,

00:04:37.420 --> 00:04:40.740
which carried more than
12 million Africans,

00:04:40.740 --> 00:04:44.630
many hundreds and even
thousands of blacksmiths

00:04:44.630 --> 00:04:45.807
were among them.

00:04:49.550 --> 00:04:54.080
They came with their knowledge
of iron working and smelting

00:04:54.080 --> 00:04:58.003
and forging and their ritual power.

00:04:59.160 --> 00:05:02.130
They were remarkable individuals
and they became leaders

00:05:02.130 --> 00:05:03.520
in the community.

00:05:03.520 --> 00:05:05.380
So on plantations,

00:05:05.380 --> 00:05:09.360
they might have been
responsible for shoeing horses,

00:05:09.360 --> 00:05:14.360
for keeping the operations of
the industrial complex going.

00:05:15.010 --> 00:05:18.320
They not only forged iron shackles,

00:05:18.320 --> 00:05:21.960
but they forged the weapons that account

00:05:21.960 --> 00:05:24.400
for the resistance to slavery.

00:05:24.400 --> 00:05:27.810
For example, we know
that African blacksmiths

00:05:27.810 --> 00:05:32.073
played a tremendous role
in the Haitian Revolution.

00:05:33.000 --> 00:05:35.916
Sometime around the late 19th century,

00:05:35.916 --> 00:05:40.916
science began to transform and
professionalize iron working

00:05:42.780 --> 00:05:43.930
in that process.

00:05:43.930 --> 00:05:47.830
Many African-Americans were
left out of the picture.

00:05:47.830 --> 00:05:51.630
They were being excluded
and discriminated against

00:05:51.630 --> 00:05:53.570
in the workplace.

00:05:53.570 --> 00:05:56.790
They still played a critical
role, and their labor,

00:05:56.790 --> 00:05:59.810
and their expertise was really important

00:05:59.810 --> 00:06:02.908
to the rise of many different industries.

00:06:04.230 --> 00:06:08.730
Today, blacksmithing has
become an important art form,

00:06:08.730 --> 00:06:12.630
and African-Americans
have a very important role

00:06:12.630 --> 00:06:16.610
in the contemporary community of artisans.

00:06:16.610 --> 00:06:21.610
They have persisted in part
because they stayed longer

00:06:21.762 --> 00:06:24.240
in rural communities.

00:06:24.240 --> 00:06:27.330
So African-American
blacksmiths have really

00:06:27.330 --> 00:06:31.760
remained critical to the
persistence of these techniques

00:06:31.760 --> 00:06:35.690
of forging, by hand,
exquisitely beautiful objects

00:06:35.690 --> 00:06:37.023
out of iron.

00:06:40.290 --> 00:06:43.363
So next I'll be grinding
the knife to shape.

00:06:45.480 --> 00:06:48.990
I take a template and I
lay it on to the billet.

00:06:48.990 --> 00:06:51.110
And then now I'm cutting the excess away.

00:06:51.110 --> 00:06:53.797
So basically the whole
perimeter of the knife.

00:07:00.360 --> 00:07:01.860
After I cut the excess away,

00:07:01.860 --> 00:07:05.240
now it's time to normalize
it and heat treat it.

00:07:05.240 --> 00:07:07.500
We did all this hard work in the beginning

00:07:07.500 --> 00:07:09.850
to get the width and
the thickness we want.

00:07:09.850 --> 00:07:11.960
So it has a lot of stress inside of it.

00:07:11.960 --> 00:07:14.033
So I need to basically massage.

00:07:14.033 --> 00:07:16.300
Basically it needs to go into the sauna.

00:07:16.300 --> 00:07:18.250
So that's what normalizing is.

00:07:18.250 --> 00:07:19.840
Heating it to it's critical temperature.

00:07:19.840 --> 00:07:22.583
And for this instance,
it's about 1,500 degrees.

00:07:25.070 --> 00:07:27.420
And then I have to quickly dip it in oil.

00:07:27.420 --> 00:07:29.070
This process is called quenching.

00:07:30.690 --> 00:07:33.380
This process is allowing
the grain structures

00:07:33.380 --> 00:07:36.460
inside the steel to
realign and mesh together,

00:07:36.460 --> 00:07:38.040
and I need it to stay that way.

00:07:38.040 --> 00:07:40.140
So that's what the quenching process does.

00:07:41.260 --> 00:07:43.020
Now since I quenched it in oil,

00:07:43.020 --> 00:07:46.320
this knife is so brittle
that I can take a hammer

00:07:46.320 --> 00:07:47.480
or throw it on the ground

00:07:47.480 --> 00:07:50.130
and it will shatter in a thousand pieces.

00:07:50.130 --> 00:07:53.420
So now I need to put it
in an oven at 400 degrees

00:07:53.420 --> 00:07:57.520
for a two hour span, allowing
it to soften just slightly,

00:07:57.520 --> 00:08:00.280
but still be hard enough to hold an edge.

00:08:00.280 --> 00:08:03.930
And that is the key point of a knife.

00:08:03.930 --> 00:08:06.294
It needs to be able to
hold an edge and stay sharp

00:08:06.294 --> 00:08:07.733
for a very long time.

00:08:12.360 --> 00:08:14.260
As a child, I was very adventurous.

00:08:14.260 --> 00:08:15.750
I was a loner.

00:08:15.750 --> 00:08:16.920
I would play in the woods,

00:08:16.920 --> 00:08:19.400
make my own bow and arrows, slingshots.

00:08:19.400 --> 00:08:21.070
I would take tubing off my swing set

00:08:21.070 --> 00:08:23.903
and flatten it with a hammer
and make my own sword.

00:08:25.670 --> 00:08:27.050
There are not many,

00:08:27.050 --> 00:08:29.460
probably five or six black knife makers

00:08:29.460 --> 00:08:32.140
that I can think of right now.

00:08:32.140 --> 00:08:36.230
And it was very hard because
it's a white dominated area.

00:08:36.230 --> 00:08:39.343
And a lot of people didn't
want to teach me starting out.

00:08:42.380 --> 00:08:46.150
I worked at a local mall
and I sold knives and cigars

00:08:46.150 --> 00:08:47.080
and swords.

00:08:47.080 --> 00:08:50.473
And a man came in one day,
his name is Jason Knight.

00:08:51.340 --> 00:08:52.920
Quintin was behind the counter.

00:08:52.920 --> 00:08:54.640
And he knew what he was talking about.

00:08:54.640 --> 00:08:55.550
He knew all the knives.

00:08:55.550 --> 00:08:56.680
He knew what they were made out of.

00:08:56.680 --> 00:08:57.840
He knew where they were from.

00:08:57.840 --> 00:09:01.750
He's very interested in the
whole idea of making knives.

00:09:01.750 --> 00:09:04.340
And he started telling me that
he make knives for living.

00:09:04.340 --> 00:09:07.583
And as a 17 year old boy,
like my eyes like brighten up,

00:09:07.583 --> 00:09:09.633
like can you teach me?

00:09:11.071 --> 00:09:12.540
He's making me nervous.

00:09:12.540 --> 00:09:13.795
Why?

00:09:13.795 --> 00:09:15.657
He's my teacher.

00:09:15.657 --> 00:09:17.563
Got to make sure I do it right.

00:09:18.960 --> 00:09:21.630
He would come to my shop and
he would show me what he made

00:09:21.630 --> 00:09:23.320
and I would give him some pointers,

00:09:23.320 --> 00:09:26.300
and then he would go back
and work on that again.

00:09:26.300 --> 00:09:30.890
And one day, he came over and he said,

00:09:30.890 --> 00:09:32.540
God told me to make chefs knives.

00:09:33.460 --> 00:09:35.120
I was following suit behind Jason,

00:09:35.120 --> 00:09:37.810
making hunting knives, swords

00:09:37.810 --> 00:09:40.450
and the fantasy movie swords.

00:09:40.450 --> 00:09:42.700
And then I had a dream.

00:09:42.700 --> 00:09:44.830
I'm very big in my faith.

00:09:44.830 --> 00:09:47.943
And the holy spirit told me
plainly, make chef knives.

00:09:49.530 --> 00:09:50.363
And I woke up.

00:09:50.363 --> 00:09:52.483
I was like, that makes sense.

00:09:53.650 --> 00:09:55.830
Probably within eight months,

00:09:55.830 --> 00:10:00.140
every chef in Charleston had
a chef's knife in their hands

00:10:00.140 --> 00:10:03.200
that Quintin had the guts
to go out there and say,

00:10:03.200 --> 00:10:06.380
I want to let you try this knife
out and see what you think.

00:10:06.380 --> 00:10:08.740
And he built a whole business around that.

00:10:08.740 --> 00:10:10.430
That's the exciting thing.

00:10:10.430 --> 00:10:12.760
That's the fun thing is
to watch something grow

00:10:12.760 --> 00:10:14.580
from a little seed

00:10:14.580 --> 00:10:16.580
and watch it become awesome.

00:10:16.580 --> 00:10:18.350
I just, I love the guy.

00:10:18.350 --> 00:10:19.183
He's my friend.

00:10:19.183 --> 00:10:20.016
He's my brother.

00:10:20.016 --> 00:10:22.713
I want him to be the rockstar
knife maker that he is.

00:10:26.330 --> 00:10:27.860
So now next what we'll be doing,

00:10:27.860 --> 00:10:30.640
we've finished doing the bevel grinding,

00:10:30.640 --> 00:10:32.813
basically getting it to a sharp point.

00:10:35.040 --> 00:10:38.320
So I'm grinding it from the
thickness that it is now

00:10:38.320 --> 00:10:40.579
to basically down to zero on the edge.

00:10:45.060 --> 00:10:47.330
I don't measure anything,
so it's all about feel.

00:10:47.330 --> 00:10:51.130
I've been doing this 18
years, and it's muscle memory.

00:10:51.130 --> 00:10:52.820
I'm constantly checking it and making sure

00:10:52.820 --> 00:10:56.660
that my grinds are
straight and consistent,

00:10:56.660 --> 00:10:58.510
make sure that everything flows well.

00:10:59.350 --> 00:11:03.620
And then I will be putting
it inside a developer.

00:11:03.620 --> 00:11:05.170
Right now it looks silver,

00:11:05.170 --> 00:11:07.250
so you really don't see the patterns yet

00:11:07.250 --> 00:11:10.200
until I put it in this ferric chloride.

00:11:10.200 --> 00:11:14.530
Now you about see what we all
wanted to see, the pattern,

00:11:14.530 --> 00:11:18.740
the twist and the turns and
the curls and the swooshes.

00:11:18.740 --> 00:11:19.999
Doesn't it look sexy?

00:11:22.900 --> 00:11:27.440
Now here comes the heavy part
for me, adding the handle.

00:11:27.440 --> 00:11:30.420
The handle material is going to be oak,

00:11:30.420 --> 00:11:32.490
but what's so special about this oak,

00:11:32.490 --> 00:11:34.930
this old came from a plantation.

00:11:34.930 --> 00:11:37.130
This plantation is
called Middleton's Place.

00:11:40.910 --> 00:11:45.110
We are currently at
Middleton's Place Plantation,

00:11:45.110 --> 00:11:48.560
maybe 20 minutes away
from Downtown Charleston.

00:11:48.560 --> 00:11:50.000
And my last name is Middleton.

00:11:50.000 --> 00:11:52.713
So, you know the deal.

00:11:55.630 --> 00:11:57.480
My ancestors were here.

00:11:57.480 --> 00:12:00.180
They worked this land.

00:12:00.180 --> 00:12:01.013
They bled here.

00:12:01.013 --> 00:12:02.093
They died here.

00:12:03.040 --> 00:12:04.630
They may were beaten here.

00:12:04.630 --> 00:12:05.873
They may were hung here.

00:12:07.510 --> 00:12:10.230
This oak tree is basically a live oak

00:12:10.230 --> 00:12:12.550
or in Charleston, an angel Oak.

00:12:12.550 --> 00:12:15.093
This tree has been here
since slavery time.

00:12:16.730 --> 00:12:19.475
The director's over
Middleton Place Plantation,

00:12:19.475 --> 00:12:21.550
we had this little project.

00:12:21.550 --> 00:12:23.340
I made some oyster knives for them

00:12:23.340 --> 00:12:26.770
and they were kind enough
to give me some of the wood

00:12:26.770 --> 00:12:28.850
that fell off this tree.

00:12:28.850 --> 00:12:31.393
And that's the handle
I used for this knife.

00:12:32.920 --> 00:12:36.453
What I want to do is
build off broken pieces.

00:12:37.790 --> 00:12:41.020
I'm taking something that's barbaric,

00:12:41.020 --> 00:12:44.430
that's a stain on American history

00:12:44.430 --> 00:12:46.933
and building off of that in my own way.

00:12:51.590 --> 00:12:53.290
This wood is basically a block

00:12:53.290 --> 00:12:55.664
and I'm going to cut it to shape.

00:13:01.360 --> 00:13:04.450
I want to create a chemical
and a mechanical bond.

00:13:04.450 --> 00:13:07.210
The chemical bond is the epoxy,

00:13:07.210 --> 00:13:09.240
the mechanical bond is a screw.

00:13:09.240 --> 00:13:11.973
And I can screw the handle to the blade.

00:13:13.000 --> 00:13:14.530
After we let the glue dry,

00:13:14.530 --> 00:13:17.460
we have to grind the
perimeter of the handle.

00:13:17.460 --> 00:13:19.950
Basically, I'm just trying to
get that coke bottle shape.

00:13:19.950 --> 00:13:21.270
I just want it to be smooth.

00:13:21.270 --> 00:13:24.200
I want it to be almost like a car.

00:13:24.200 --> 00:13:25.180
The lines need to fit.

00:13:25.180 --> 00:13:26.653
They need to flow just right.

00:13:28.170 --> 00:13:30.510
And I want to add a sealer.

00:13:30.510 --> 00:13:33.150
Now you about to see the rings of the oak.

00:13:33.150 --> 00:13:36.840
You can almost count and see
how old this tree really was.

00:13:36.840 --> 00:13:39.270
Now it's time to sharpen the knife.

00:13:39.270 --> 00:13:41.700
And I'm paying very close
attention on the angle,

00:13:41.700 --> 00:13:45.083
making sure that it's not
too steep or not too shallow.

00:13:46.490 --> 00:13:48.820
Now you're seeing how
sexy this knife look.

00:13:48.820 --> 00:13:50.990
I am proud to put my name on it.

00:13:50.990 --> 00:13:54.020
So now I'll be putting my
maker's mark on it, my logo.

00:13:54.020 --> 00:13:55.610
I use a laser engraver.

00:13:55.610 --> 00:13:59.300
And will be allowing to
laser to engrave my logo

00:13:59.300 --> 00:14:00.223
inside this knife.

00:14:01.570 --> 00:14:05.197
Growing up, my granddad told
me your name is everything.

00:14:05.197 --> 00:14:09.170
To put your name on something
that means you stand by it,

00:14:09.170 --> 00:14:10.180
just like your handshake.

00:14:10.180 --> 00:14:11.630
Your stand by your handshake.

00:14:15.370 --> 00:14:18.803
Now it's time to box it up,
wrap it up and ship it out.

00:14:19.750 --> 00:14:21.730
Since this one is so special,

00:14:21.730 --> 00:14:25.090
and it's so much emotion inside this one,

00:14:25.090 --> 00:14:26.790
it will probably be around $2,000.

00:14:40.080 --> 00:14:43.008
People ask me a lot, how long
does it take to make a knife?

00:14:43.008 --> 00:14:45.490
I answer that two ways.

00:14:45.490 --> 00:14:47.980
If I have everything on hand,

00:14:47.980 --> 00:14:50.013
I can make that knife in eight hours.

00:14:51.140 --> 00:14:53.963
And the second way I answer
that is kind of snobbish.

00:14:54.920 --> 00:14:55.853
18 years.

00:14:57.594 --> 00:14:59.403
You have to look off at it.

00:15:02.040 --> 00:15:04.510
The time and the mistakes that I've made

00:15:04.510 --> 00:15:07.610
and all the blood, sweat, and
tears I put in to my craft,

00:15:07.610 --> 00:15:09.310
it feels like second nature to me.

00:15:11.430 --> 00:15:13.020
Growing up as a boy,

00:15:13.020 --> 00:15:16.000
you never really hear like
you're doing a good job.

00:15:16.000 --> 00:15:19.970
As a southern man, you more
like go to school, go to work,

00:15:19.970 --> 00:15:22.380
work hard, provide for your family.

00:15:22.380 --> 00:15:26.520
But to build this knife
in honor of my ancestors,

00:15:26.520 --> 00:15:28.053
they're saying job well done.

00:15:29.090 --> 00:15:30.100
I've built my brand.

00:15:30.100 --> 00:15:33.170
I've built this company.

00:15:33.170 --> 00:15:35.000
And what I'm hearing in my spirit,

00:15:35.000 --> 00:15:37.700
and what I'm hearing in
my inner man is saying,

00:15:37.700 --> 00:15:39.130
I'm proud of you.

00:15:39.130 --> 00:15:39.963
That's what I feel.

